Abstract
Innovative solid-phase adsorbent is needed urgently for highly efficient extraction of mercury $(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II}))$ from aqueous solution. Herein, highly porous inorganic materials, including $\mathrm{zrOx}$ , ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf, were obtained from topotactic transformations of metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) via a facile ligand extraction process, which preserved the original MOF morphology and introduced useful functionalities. ZrOx and ZrOxyPhos exhibited high adsorption efficiency for $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ . And ZrSulf showed fastest adsorption kinetics $(1.1{\times}10^{-2}~\mathrm{g}/(\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{min}))$ and highest adsorption capacity $(824~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ among the obtained three inorganic materials due to the presence of accessible sulfur-based functionality, which surpassed most of other benchmark porous adsorbents. Moreover, ZrSulf showed good regeneration ability and excellent selectivity for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ in the presence of other coexisting metal ions, such as Cd(II), Ni(II), Co(II), Mn(II) and Zn(II), and other inorganic cations, such as $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ . The excellent adsorption performance of ZrSulf towards $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ can be traceable to the covalent bond formation with sulfur-based functionality combined with ion exchange with Na. Given its low-cost and easy preparation nature, ZrSulf could be potentially valuable candidates for the elimination of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ from aqueous media. Changing the MOF templates and using different digest solutions will introduce different metal and structural possibilities, which demonstrates the potential of such ligand extraction method for synthesizing porous inorganic adsorbents for heavy metal ion decontamination.
Keywords: Metal-organic frameworks, Porous inorganic materials, $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption, Selectivity
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are structurally porous crystalline solids constructed from organic linkers and metal nodes that can be either metal clusters or individual metals. Nowadays, MOFs have vast potential in many applications including catalysis [1, 2], gas storage and separations [3, 4], molecular sensing [5, 6] and many others [7-11] due to their structural diversity endowed by limitless choice of bridging ligands with different symmetry, length and functionality. The strength of their coordination bond between the bridging linkers and secondary building units (SBUs) is usually relatively weak, which is a drawback in many potential applications, while it is an advantage for creating novel materials due to the modifiable structure and modest chemical stability.
This intrinsic characteristic has been proved to show great superiority in several promising applications. For example, nano MOFs can act as efficient delivery vehicles for drugs and imaging contrast agents [12, 13], which benefits from the fragile metal−ligand bond. Besides, after the removal of SBUs from MOFs, MOFs can be also exploited as precursors for the synthesis of porous carbon or cubic cross-linked polymers as bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst or supercapacitor electrodes [14, 15]. Conversely, by removing the bridging organic ligands from MOFs, MOFs can be applied as precursors for the fabrication of inorganic materials with high surface area and various possible pore sizes. On the basis of various MOFs available, a lot of inorganic material nanostructures, such as ${\bf a}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [16], $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ for supercapacitors [17], $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for CO oxidation [18], ZnO for lithium ion conversion [19], and $\mathtt{C u O}$ for lithium-ion storage [20], have now been obtained with the benefit of various MOF availability as precursors. And more and more novel inorganic materials with new properties and extensional abilities are anticipated by using MOFs as sacrificial templates. Desperately, the high-priced carbonization reactions hinder their batch preparation and large-scale applications, although MOFs are promising precursors for synthesizing new materials. An inspiring breakthrough in preparing inorganic materials by using MOFs as sacrificial templates has recently been obtained by Lin et al.[21, 22] Robust indium sulfide and zirconium phosphate with high adsorptive efficiency for heavy metal ions were prepared by a simple ligand extraction method, which permits rapid and large-scale adsorbent preparation under mild conditions without high-priced carbonization process, complicated ligand syntheses, or challenging activation steps.
Herein, highly defective UiO-66 $\mathrm{(Zr_{6}(O H)_{4}O_{4}(B D C)_{6}},$ BDC = Benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate) was exploited as sacrificial templates to fabricate three kinds of stable and porous inorganic materials via facile ligand extraction process. Similar to postsynthetic modification [23], postsynthetic exchange [24] and metal-ion exchange [25], different functional inorganic materials can be fabricated from MOF precursors by removing the organic ligands or substituting with other inorganic moieties added in the digest solution. Differing from porous metal oxides obtained by costly carbonization process, various final materials possessing delicate surface functionalities can be achieved by using different extraction solutions. The obtained inorganic materials also retain high surface area, porosity and preserve the morphology of the original MOFs. The high stability and porosity of these inorganic materials endows them ideally applicable in wastewater contamination.
Mercury $\left({\mathrm{Hg}}\right)$ contamination can induce brain damage, birth defects and disease to humans and other species, which is a threat to public health and aquatic life for a long time [26]. Hg release into aqueous media mainly originates from industry process and chemical byproducts including chemicals, electronic materials, batteries and fossil fuel combustion. Recently, a global agreement has been established to reduce the $\mathrm{Hg}$ threat, which stimulates researchers to remove and recover Hg from contaminated aqueous media [27]. An acceptable limit of 2 ppb for $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\mathrm{Hg}}$ in drinking water has been strictly regulated by the United States Environmental Protection Agency, and a much lower limit has been mandated for the release of $\mathrm{Hg}$ into ecosystems [28]. Therefore, rapid and efficient decontamination of large amounts of $\mathrm{Hg}$ -contaminated wastewater is crucial to environmental remediation.
Herein we explored the use of the three porous inorganic materials transformed from defective UiO-66 as an alternative technology for the removal of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ from aqueous solutions. The prepared inorganic materials exhibited enhanced removal efficiency for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ in both capacity and kinetics, which surpassed most of other porous materials and showed the great potential for heavy metal decontamination.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
$\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}$ was purchased from J&K Scientific (Beijing, China). Sodium sulfide nonahydrate $\mathrm{(Na_{2}S\cdot9H_{2}O)}$ was provide by Beijing John Lunda CO., LTD. (Beijing,
China). Other chemicals were obtained from Aladdin or Sigma Aldrich. Chemicals and solvents were used without further purification. UiO-66-50Benz was synthesized according to the previous procedure [29]. UiO-66-50Benz was referred to the resulting UiO-66 sample which was prepared with the extra addition of a 50-fold excess of benzoic acid as a modulator in the synthesis process of UiO-66.
## 2.2. Transformation of MOFs to inorganic adsorbents
$100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of UiO-66-50Benz was suspended in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of water and then $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of 10 mol/L NaOH solution $(Z\mathrm{rOx})$ or 210 mM $\mathrm{Na_{3}P O_{4}}$ solution (ZrOxyPhos) was added into the UiO-66-50Benz suspension. For the preparation of ZrSulf, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of UiO-66 was added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water containing $250~\mathrm{mg}$ of ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}{\cdot}9{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . These suspensions were agitated overnight, and the remaining white solids were collected after centrifugation and purified with water. Note that UiO-66-50Benz treated with $\mathrm{Na_{3}P O_{4}}$ was further washed with N, N-dimethyl formamide $\mathrm{(C_{3}H_{7}N O)}$ to remove the terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(C_{8}H_{6}O_{4})}$ and benzoic acid $\mathrm{\cdotC_{6}H_{5}C O O H})$ ), both of which were added in the synthesis process of UiO-66-50Benz.
## 2.3. Characterization
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained on a Hitachi S4800 microscope operated at an accelerating voltage of 6.0 kV. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) observations were performed on a FEI Tecnai G2 F20 S-Twin working at $200~\mathrm{kV}.$ Energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectra were obtained by combining a scanning tunneling microscope with an energy-dispersive X-ray detector. Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a diffractometer (model X’Pert Pro; PANalytical, Spectris plc, Almelo, The Netherlands) with Cu Kα radiation $\langle\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Fourier-transformed infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was achieved using a Nicolet 6700 FTIR spectrometer (Thermo Electron Corporation, Madison, WI) with MCT (mercury cadmium telluride) detector in the resolution of $4~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherms were obtained at 77 K using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020M system and calculated using the Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET) model in the range of $0.005<\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}<0.1$ . The samples were outgassed at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{{C}}$ for 12 h before the measurements. X-ray photon spectroscopy (XPS) were performed by an ESCALAB 250 Xi XPS system of Thermo Scientific, where the analysis chamber was $\boldsymbol{1.5\times10^{-9}}$ mbar and the X-ray spot was 500 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed in air using a Shimadzu TGA-50 and a Shimadzu TGA-50H with a platinum pan, heated at a rate of $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ . The quantification of mercury species and other metal ions in supernatant was determined by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) (PerkinElmer-SCIEX, Thornhill, ON, Canada). The compositions of ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf were determined by using ICP-MS and CHN Analysis.
## 2.4. Batch adsorption experiments
All adsorption experiments were carried out at $25^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ . Initially, 2.0, 5.0, 10.0 mg of ZrSulf were suspended into $10.0\mathrm{\mL\Hg^{2+}}$ solution with the concentration of 10.0 m $\mathrm{\underline{{\s}}/L\ (p H=6.8\ N a H_{2}P O_{4}/N a_{2}H P O_{4}}$ buffer, 2 mmol/L). The solubility product $(K_{\mathrm{sp}})$ for $(\mathrm{Hg}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}$ is $(4.0{\pm}0.3)\times\mathrm{l0}^{-22}[\$ [30]. The adsorption efficiency of ZrSulf for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ was ca. 60, 99.1 and $100\%$ for the ZrSulf dosage of 0.2, 0.5 and $1.0\ \mathrm{g/L}$ , respectively.
For the sake of saving materials, the ZrSulf dosage was fixed as $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ . For $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ adsorption kinetics, $5.0\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbents were suspended into $10.0\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ solutions $\mathrm{(pH=6.8NaH_{2}P O_{4}/N a_{2}H P O_{4}}$ buffer, 2 mmol/L) with the concentration of $10.0\mathrm{mg/L}$ . After the adsorption proceeds for a scheduled time, the supernatants were separated from the adsorbents by centrifugation (5000 rpm, 5 min) and analyzed by ICP-MS. For the adsorption experiments affected by solution pH, 5.0 mg of ZrSulf was suspended into $10.0\mathrm{\mL\Hg^{2+}}$ solution with the concentration of $10.0~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . The solution $\mathsf{p H}$ was adjusted by using dilute HCl and NaOH aqueous solution. For adsorption isotherm tests, the initial $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ concentration was varied from 10 to 500 $\mathrm{mg/L}$ with the ZrSulf dosage of $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ , and the mixture was shaken for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ to reach the adsorption equilibrium. The adsorption capacity $(q_{\mathrm{e}},\ \mathrm{mg/g)}$ of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ was calculated using the following equation:
$$
q_{\mathrm{e}}=(C_{0}-C_{\mathrm{e}})\times V/m
$$
where $C_{0}$ $\mathrm{\mg/L)}$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ $\mathrm{\langlemg/L\rangle}$ is the initial and equilibrium concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ respectively. V is the volume of the $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ solution $(\mathrm{L})$ , and $m$ is the weight of adsorbents (g).
For the selectivity experiments, $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of adsorbents was suspended into $10.0~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ solutions $\mathrm{\Delta\Psi(pH=6.8}$ $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{4}/N a_{2}H P O_{4}}$ buffer, $2{\mathrm{~mmol/L}}$ ) with the concentration of $10.0~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ . Another competitive adsorption experiments were conducted as the initial $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ concentration was fixed at $10.0~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , while the coexisting inorganic ion (including $\mathrm{{Na}^{+},\mathrm{{K}^{+},\mathrm{{Mg}^{2+}}}}$ and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ ) concentration was fixed at 10.0 or $1000.0\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ . The ZrSulf dosage was set as $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ and solution pH was adjusted to 6.8. After $120\mathrm{min}$ of adsorption, the $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ concentration in the supernatant was determined after centrifugation.
The regeneration of the adsorbents was carried out by using 0.01 M HCl + 0.1% thiourea according to the previous published work [31]. Typically, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of ZrSulf was shaken with $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of $10\mathrm{mg/L}\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ solutions in a conical flask for 90 min at 25 $^\mathrm{{{\circ}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ . After $90~\mathrm{min}$ , ZrSulf was separated and washed with Milli-Q water to eliminate the excess of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions. Then, $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ -loaded ZrSulf was treated with 0.01 M HCl $^+$ $0.1\%$ thiourea and reused.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of inorganic adsorbents
Scheme 1 Depiction of the wet-treatment process to generate inorganic adsorbents rom sacrificial UiO-66-50Bens.
To obtained robust porous adsorbents, the MOF template UiO-66-50Benz was selected as precursors because it not only requires ordinary organic ligands (terephthalic acid and benzoic acid) and possesses stable SBUs consisting of metal-oxo clusters, but also contains a very high concentration of missing cluster defects, which have a positive effect on the adsorption applications [32, 33].
Characterization by SEM reveals distinct crystals for octahedral UiO-66-50Benz (Fig.
1). The identity of UiO-66-50Benz was further confirmed by PXRD, TGA and BET techniques, which are consistent with previous reports [29]. UiO-66-50Benz was suspended overnight in the reaction solution of NaOH, $\mathrm{Na_{3}P O_{4}}$ and ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}{\cdot}9{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ Driving by thermodynamics, these organic linkers in UiO-66-50Benz could be removed via a ligand exchange process followed by collection to obtain porous oxide $(\mathrm{ZrOx})$ , phosphate (ZrOxyPhos) and sulfide (ZrSulf) adsorbents. The yield was up to 70, 60 and $88\%$ for ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf, respectively. Scheme 1 depicts that the metal atoms in the SBUs linked together with inorganic bridging groups exist in the digestion solution.
(D).
SEM images reveal that the obtained inorganic materials reserved the morphology of the original UiO-66-50Benz (Fig. 1). The surface of the obtained inorganic materials was irregular with respect to the original framework, which could be due to the creation of pores during the ligand extraction reaction. TEM images reveal that the dimensions of ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf were found to be a little smaller than that of UiO-66-50Benz precursor, which is attributed to the removal of the bridging organic ligands (Fig. S1).
In contrast to that of original UiO-66-50Benz, TGA measurements of $Z\mathrm{rOx}.$ ， ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf reveal no other weight loss besides residual solvent evaporation (Fig. 2A), suggesting that the organic linkers were removed thoroughly during the ligand extraction process. No weight gain was observed with the increasing of temperatures in the TGA measurements of ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf, namely, the remaining weight of ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf decreased with increasing temperature, suggesting the formation of ZrOxyPhos or ZrSulf materials instead of ZrOx. There were no obvious peaks appeared in the XRD patterns of ZrOxyPhos, ZrSulf and ZrOx (Fig. 2B), which suggested that the crystallinity of UiO-66-50Benz was lost completely and revealed that the obtained inorganic adsorbents were amorphous. The loss of crystallinity was mainly due to the extraction of terephthalic acid and benzoic acid by using the extraction solvents such as ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}$ , NaOH or $\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ . Other researchers also found the similar experimental phenomena [21, 22]. Additional characterizations were carried out by FTIR technique to validate the incorporation of functionalities. In the FTIR spectra of all the obtained materials, a broad O-H stretching peak located at around $3400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is observed which can be traceable to the remaining Zr–OH groups in the original SBUs of UiO-66-50Benz (Fig. 2C). It is reported that the bands corresponding to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups cannot be found in $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ calcined from MOFs [34]. The FTIR spectrum of ZrOxyPhos (Fig. 2D) reveals a stretching peak located at around $3485~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is corresponded to PO−H stretching [34]. The clearly visible strong band present from 1200 to $1000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the combination of $\mathrm{\bfP-O}$ stretching and P−OH stretching [34]. The incorporation of surface thiol functionalities was validated by FTIR results where weak S–H stretching peak was present in ZrSulf around $2400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 2E) [21].
Composition analysis of ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf suggested that ca. 3.5 wt. $\%$ of $\mathrm{\bfP}$ or 4.2 wt. $\%$ of S was introduced into ZrOxyPhos or ZrSulf. Due to the relatively low amount of S, the intensity of S–H stretching bond was not very strong. Thus the limitation of this method was the low doping ability. However, this method is facile and cheap. The XPS results revealed the elements existed in the obtained porous inorganic materials (Fig. 2F).
${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desoprtion isotherms were applied to explore the pore sizes and surface areas of the obtained inorganic materials. BET results (Fig. 3A) reveal that the porous structure was retained by the ligand extraction process. ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf have a surface area of 430, 290 and $560~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, which are comparable to pillared $Z\mathbf{r}$ -phosphonates $(250-440~\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ [35] and zirconium oxophosphates $(468~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ [36]. As shown in Fig. 3B, ZrOx and ZrOxyPhos have pores about 14 and $34\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ , respectively. And ZrSulf has pores about 12 and $27\textup{\AA}$ . All these inorganic materials show type IV isotherm shape and possess hysteresis loops because of capillary condensation within pores, which suggests the existence of mesopores. The hydrated ionic radii of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ is $4.22\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ [37]. These mesopores could promote the diffusion of adsorbates into the inorganic adsorbents and make their functionality more accessible.
experiment data, solid lines represent Langmuir model fitting, and dashed lines represent Freundlich model fitting; adsorption curve of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ versus contact time in aqueous solution by ZrSulf fitted by nonlinear regression analysis (B).
Given the high porosity and new functionality introduced to these materials, UiO-66-50Benz precursor, ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf were exploited to explore the ability of extracting $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ from aqueous solutions. From Fig. 4A, ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf exhibited enhanced adsorption efficiency for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ with respect to UiO-66-50Benz. The adsorption data were fitted by the Langmuir model that is often appropriate for a homogeneous surface on the assumption of all the adsorption sites with equal affinity towards adsorbates [38] and can be expressed as:
$$
C_{s}=\frac{b C_{s\operatorname*{max}}C_{e}}{1+b C_{e}}
$$
$$
\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{s}}=\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{F}}\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}^{\mathrm{'}}
$$
where $K_{\mathrm{F}}\ (\mathrm{mg}^{1-\mathrm{n}}{\cdot}(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})^{1/\mathrm{n}})$ is the adsorption capacity when the equilibrium $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ concentration equals to 1, and $n$ is the degree of adsorption dependence with equilibrium concentration. As displayed in Fig. 4A and Table S1, the Langmuir model fitted the experimental data well, while fits performed by the Freundlich model had relatively lower $R^{2}$ values for all inorganic materials, suggesting that the Langmuir model was more representative of the observed adsorption behaviors. As shown Table S1, all the adsorption capacities of ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos or ZrSulf were higher than that of UiO-66-50Benz, disclosing the advantage for enhancing the adsorption performance for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ by using ligand extraction process for treating MOFs. Compared with ZrOx $(485~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ and ZrOxyPhos $\mathrm{(663~mg/g)}$ , ZrSulf was determined to have a adsorption capacity up to $824~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which is more than 6 times higher than thiol-laced UiO-66 [40], and much more than most of adsorbents reported in recent three years (Table 1).
Given the high adsorption capacity of ZrSulf for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ , we also evaluated its adsorption kinetics. Fig. 4B shows that after the contact time of $60~\mathrm{\min}$ , the concentration of a heavily $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ polluted solution ( $10\ \mathrm{\mg/L})$ decreased to the acceptable limit of 2 ppb for drinking water, disclosing fast $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption kinetics of ZrSulf. This forms a sharp contrast to the long contact time required for other porous adsorbents which usually needs $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to as much as several days [50-53]. The kinetic data were further fitted with the pseudo-second order kinetic model and intraparticle diffusion model which can be expressed as Eq. 3 and Eq. 4:
$$
\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{K^{\prime}q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
$$
q_{\mathrm{t}}=k_{\mathrm{p}}t^{1/2}+C
$$
where $\boldsymbol{K}$ $\mathrm{(g/(mg\min))}$ represents the pseudo-second-order rate constant, $q_{\mathrm{t}}$ $\mathrm{(mg/g)}$ represents the amount of adsorbed $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ at time $t$ (min), and $q_{\mathrm{e}}$ $(\mathrm{mg/g})$ denotes the equilibrium adsorption amount. $k_{\mathrm{p}}$ is the rate constant $\mathrm{(mg/(g\min^{1/2})}$ of intraparticle diffusion model and $C$ is a constant for any experiment $(\mathrm{mg/g)}$ . The intraparticle diffusion model was used to evaluate the adsorption mechanism [54]. The fitting results as shown in Fig. S2 suggest that the adsorption process was controlled by film diffusion and pore diffusion simultaneously. To obtain the adsorption rate, we used non-linear least squares (NLLS) adjustment of the experimental data to the pseudo-second-order kinetic equation by using Matlab software. The results are shown in Fig. 4B, the pseudo-second-order model was perfectly applicable for the adsorption kinetics of ZrSulf toward $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ , represented by fine consistencies between the calculated (21.97 mg/g) and experimental $q_{\mathrm{e}}$ values $(19.81~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ . The adsorption $\boldsymbol{K}$ value was calculated to be $1.1{\times}10^{-2}~\mathrm{g}/(\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{min})$ , which is much larger than those of other adsorbents for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ removal under similar conditions, such as $1.04{\times}10^{-3}\ \mathrm{g/(mg}$ min) for thiol modified $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}@{\bf S}\mathrm{i}{\bf O}_{2}$ [55], $5.6{\times}10^{-3}~\mathrm{g}/(\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{min})$ for SH-HMSMCs [31] and $3.9{\times}10^{-3}~\mathrm{g}/(\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{min})$ for DHPCT $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ MNPs [56]. It is well known that the property of a porous adsorbents is determined by the accessibility of binding sites as well as bonding strength [57]. The high adsorption capacity and rapid adsorption kinetics can be attributed to the synergistic effects of homogeneous distribution of soft sulfur atoms and mesopores supporting fast diffusion of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ throughout the ZrSulf.
It is well established that the adsorption behavior is dictated by the solution pH. An optimized solution pH can reduce the interference from the matrix as well as enhance the adsorption efficiency. Fig. 5A shows the adsorption efficiency can retain in a wide pH range. The $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ species under different solution $\mathsf{p H}$ values were shown in Fig. 5B. At $\mathrm{pH}<3.0$ , the dominant $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ species are $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{HgOH^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{HgCl_{2}^{+}}$ . In the solution $\mathsf{p H}$ of 3.0-9.0, the dominant $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ species are $\mathrm{Hg}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (aq), HgClOH(aq) and ${\mathrm{Hg}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . And ${\mathrm{Hg}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is the dominant $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ species at $\mathrm{pH}>9.0.$ Thus ZrSulf can remove various kinds of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ species at $\mathrm{pH}<9.0$ , and precipitation occurred at $\mathrm{pH}>9.0$ . Distribution coefficient $(K_{\mathrm{d}})$ , a mass-weighted partition coefficient denoting the affinity of adsorbates towards adsorbents, are defined by Eq. 5:
$$
K_{d}=(\frac{C_{0}-C_{e}}{C_{e}})\times\frac{V}{m}
$$
The $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ is an important metric for evaluating the performance of adsorbents. Generally, $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values above ${10}^{4}$ are very well, meanwhile values above ${{10}^{5}}$ are considered as particularly excellent [58, 59]. In this study, ZrSulf demonstrated a high $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ value of $4.98\times10^{5}$ mL/g (Fig. 5B) at $\mathrm{pH}6.8$ . Fig. 5B compares the $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values and maximum adsorption capacities of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ by various high-performance adsorbents [21, 28, 40, 59-62]. Notably, ZrSulf exhibits a high adsorption capacity and large $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ value.
3.3. Selectivity and stability of adsorbents after the adsorption of $H g(I I)$
The selectivity of an adsorbent is of great importance for evaluating its adsorption performance in the practical applications. The influence of background metal ions on the removal of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ by ZrSulf from wastewater were investigated in a $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ solution containing Cd(II), Co(II), Cu(II), Ni(II), Pb(II), Mn(II) and $Z\mathrm{n(II)}$ ions as well as $\mathrm{As(V)}$ oxyanions. Fig. 6A shows that the adsorption efficiency of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ by
ZrSulf is more than $99\%$ , and about 25, 20 and $15\%$ of $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ , $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathrm{As(V)}$ were removed while no apparent removal efficiency of other background metal cations was observed. This phenomenon discloses that ZrSulf can selectively remove $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ from aqueous media in spite of the interfering metal ions. The high selectivity of ZrSulf for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ against other interfering metal ions such as Cd(II), Co(II), Ni(II), Mn(II) and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ions should be ascribed to the strong soft–soft interactions between the sulfur-based functionality within $Z\mathrm{r}S\mathrm{ulf}$ and Hg(II)/Pb(II)/Cu(II) ions.
Given the high concentration of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ and coexisting inorganic ions (e.g. $\mathrm{\DeltaNa}^{+},\mathrm{\DeltaK}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ ) in wastewater [48], the effect of these conventional inorganic ions on adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ was carried out. As shown in Fig. S3, it was found that the coexisting $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ just slightly inhibited the adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ onto ZrSulf, as the adsorption efficiency only decreased by $4.5~\%$ and $6.0\%$ even at an inorganic ion concentration of $1000~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ . The inhibition effects caused by the divalent ions $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ ) were larger than those by monovalent ions. However, large $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ adsorption efficiency of 80 and $78\%$ (decreased by $18\%$ and $20\%$ ) was still obtained in the presence of $1000~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ . This phenomenon suggested that the uptake of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ by ZrSulf was via a mechanism of ion exchange.
The regeneration of ZrSulf achieved successfully via a solvent desorption process. Fig. 6B shows that the adsorption capacities have no apparent loss after 5 cycles of adsorption and desorption, disclosing that ZrSulf can be regenerated and is appropriate for wastewater decontamination. Meanwhile, the TEM image reveals that the morphology of ZrSulf after regeneration was scarcely changed (Fig. S4A). All these results verified the stability and reusability of the ZrSulf in potential practical applications. The BET specific surface area of ZrSulf before adsorption was $560~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , while the surface area decreased to $450\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ after the adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ (Fig. S4B). Similarly, the decrease in the surface area has also been reported after the uptake of selenium by NU-1000 [21]. The corresponding size distribution discloses that the pore size has a little decrease after $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption (Fig. S4C).
## 3.4. Comparison with other adsorbents
It is still useful to compare the $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption performance of ZrSulf with other adsorbents. Table 1 tabulates the maximal adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ by different adsorbents reported in recent three years. In the perspective of adsorption capacity, the adsorption capacity of ZrSulf $(824~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ was more than that of most adsorbents, most of which was less than $500~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Both copper sulfide [49] and $\mathrm{H}_{1.45}\mathrm{Na}_{0.45}\mathrm{InS}_{2.45}$ [21] have higher adsorption capacities in comparison with ZrSulf, while copper sulfide couldn’t be regenerated and the precursor of $\mathrm{H}_{1.45}\mathrm{Na}_{0.45}\mathrm{InS}_{2.45}$ was not economical.
Table 2 tabulates comparisons of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption performance and synthetic methods among various reported adsorbents. In terms of $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values, the $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values of most $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorbents were low than that of ZrSulf. Zr-DMBD [40], LHMS-1 [60] and PAF-SH-1 [28] showed relatively high $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values (nearly more than $1{\times}10^{6}~\mathrm{ml/g})$ in comparison with ZrSulf $(4.98{\times}10^{5}\ \mathrm{ml/g})$ . Although LHMS-1 was inexpensive, it showed very low adsorption capacity $\mathrm{(87~mg/g)}$ , and the reusability remains unclear. The preparation of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -DMBD required costly organic ligands, and the synthesis of PAF-SH-1 required multistep reactions including modification of porous organic polymers. In contrast, ZrSulf can be prepared via a facile ligand extraction reaction. In these respects, ZrSulf is cost-effective in $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ remediation applications.
## 3.5 Evidence and mechanism for $H g(I I)$ removal
TEM-EDX analysis and mapping carried out on ZrSulf after the adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ shows that mercury element is evenly distributed throughout the framework of ZrSulf, confirming that the adsorption occurred on and in the framework (Fig. 7), while no visible morphological changes can be observed. To illustrate the removal mechanism with Hg(II), the samples of ZrSulf before and after the adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ at $\mathrm{pH}~6.8$ were characterized by using XPS technique. It is obvious that the peaks corresponding to Zr 3d, O 1s, Na 1s, C 1s, S 2p and $\mathrm{Hg}$ 4f are identified in the survey scan spectra (Fig. 8A). The peaks located at around 101 and 105 eV can be corresponded to the $\mathrm{Hg4f}_{7/2}$ and $\mathrm{Hg4f}_{5/2}$ peaks (Fig. 8B), further affirming that $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ was adsorbed onto ZrSulf. Compared with the $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ binding energies for purified $\mathrm{Hg}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ centered at $105\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{{Hg}_{\ 4f_{5/2}}}$ and $102{\mathrm{~eV}}$ for $\mathrm{{Hg}~4f_{7/2}}$ , respectively, an obvious shift of $1.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ to a lower binding energy of $\mathrm{Hg~4f}_{7/2}$ for the ZrSulf sample after $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption was observed, which reveals the formation of strong affinities between the $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ and ZrSulf. Fig. 8C shows that the binding energy for $\mathtt{S}2\mathtt{p}$ in the –SH groups of UiO-66-SH was located at $163.8\mathrm{eV},$ while the binding energy of S 2p shifted slightly to a higher value after $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption, which was traceable to the electron acquisition of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ from the S atoms in the $-S\mathrm{H}$ groups [28]. Compared with the original ZrSulf sample, the peak in the ZrSulf sample after the adsorption of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ located at $1072\mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to the Na 1s disappeared obviously (Fig. 8D), which discloses that ion-exchange between Na and $\mathrm{Hg}$ also occurs in the adsorption process. Thus, the excellent performance of ZrSulf for $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ removal can be traceable to the covalent bond formation with sulfur-based functionality combined with ion exchange with Na, which can be confirmed by the selectivity experimental results. At $\mathrm{pH}<3.0$ , the dominant adsorption mechanism was proposed to be ion exchange. And coordination between $\operatorname{Hg}(\mathrm{H})$ species and sulfur-containing groups was proposed to be the controlled adsorption mechanism at $3.0<\mathrm{pH}<9.0$ . At $\mathrm{pH}>9.0$ , precipitation was the main adsorption mechanism.
