## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, porous inorganic materials with accessible functionalities were obtained by using UiO-66 as sacrificial templates via a ligand extraction process. The obtained inorganic materials (ZrOx, ZrOxyPhos and ZrSulf) maintained the original morphology and porosity of UiO-66, and possessed new functionalities. Among the obtained three inorganic materials, ZrSulf showed fastest adsorption kinetics and highest adsorption capacity for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ due to the presence of accessible sulfur-based functionality, which surpassed most of other benchmark porous adsorbents. The prepared ZrSulf could be regenerated easily and be reused more than 5 times without obvious decrease in adsorption efficiency. Additionally, ZrSulf showed good selectivity for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ in the presence of many other coexisting metal ions. More importantly, ZrSulf showed high adsorption efficiency for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ in the presence of other inorganic cations even at 10 times concentration of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ with respect to that of $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ . This MOF template technique may show great potential for preparing highly porous adsorbents for wastewater decontamination in the future.
